		},
		{
			"id": "e_n-8CKp4kMMZxKIW",
			"description": "Mindshackle Scarabs",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A cloud of small, spider-like scarab constructs carried by some Necron Overlords, released to swarm over an enemy in close combat and briefly seize control of their nervous system; Codex: Necrons background and the tabletop's own long-standing special rule for the item both describe it forcing a struck opponent, for a moment, to act against their own side before the effect passes.\n\nDesign: Built from GURPS's own Mind Control affliction (B40) rather than a bespoke stun or damage mechanic, since \"briefly forces one action\" is exactly what that advantage already models; triggered as an automatic effect on a successful melee hit rather than requiring the bearer to declare and roll a wholly separate attack, the same \"attaches to an existing successful hit\" design this project's Aeldari Mandiblasters entry uses for its own toxin. Limiting the compelled action to one simple, GM-judged act (not a full round of enemy- directed combat) keeps a Necron Overlord from effectively removing an enemy combatant outright on a single failed Will roll, which the tabletop's own \"make one attack against a nearby unit\" framing already suggests is the right scope. Handling: 1 lb, a small swarm carried in a housing on the bearer's own limb or shoulder, not a proper weapon in its own right; ST 10 and Brawling in the weapon block are nominal, present only so the affliction note has somewhere to live.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"Necrons"
			],
			"tech_level": "12^",
			"legality_class": "0",
			"base_value": "9000",
			"base_weight": "1 lb",
			"weapons": [
				{
					"id": "wZBz0tWjdPf4spTnJ",
					"sv": 1,
					"damage": {
						"type": "spec"
					},
					"reach": "C",
					"strength": "10",
					"usage": "Touch (triggered by a successful unarmed or close-combat hit)",
					"usage_notes": "A target struck in melee by the bearer (whether or not the strike itself does any damage) must roll Will or be affected by Mind Control (B40) for 1 turn, forcing one simple action the GM judges plausible for the scarabs to compel (drop a weapon, step back, strike a nearby ally) rather than a detailed set of commands - the swarm can only seize a moment's control, not hold a conversation with the victim's own mind. A successful resistance roll makes the target immune to this specific bearer's scarabs for the rest of the encounter. No damage of its own; the weapon block exists only to give the build tool a place to attach this note, per data-format.md's schema.",
					"defaults": [
						{
							"type": "dx",
							"modifier": 0
						},
						{
							"type": "skill",
							"name": {
								"compare": "is",
								"qualifier": "Brawling"
							}
						}
					]
				}
			],
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "eZI3JlI9vYe-O6a0v",
			"description": "Veil of Darkness",
			"local_notes": "Once activated (typically once per scene; the GM may allow a second use after a long rest linked to a Tomb World's power, per this project's usual \"left to GM judgement\" treatment of a limited-use relic teleport), the bearer and every willing Necron construct within 5 yards vanish and reappear together up to 200 yards away, anywhere the bearer has previously seen or can otherwise identify with certainty - built as GURPS's own Teleport advantage (B90) applied to a group rather than the bearer alone, the same design basis this project's Imperium Displacer Field already uses for a single-target version of the same underlying mechanic. Anyone unwilling, unconscious, or simply too far from the bearer at the moment of activation is left behind.\n\nLore: A Cryptek-forged relic that lets a Necron Overlord and their immediate retinue withdraw an entire fireteam from danger (or reposition it for an ambush) in a single instant; Codex: Necrons background and Black Library's Necron fiction both use it specifically for a group withdrawal or repositioning rather than a single Necron's own personal escape, distinguishing it from the more individual teleportation effects Necron characters otherwise carry.\n\nDesign: Built on the same Teleport advantage (B90) the Imperium Displacer Field already uses, widened from a single wearer to a small group per the anchor's own \"group teleport\" instruction - the honest, minimal change needed to take an existing, already-verified design basis and apply it to more than one person at once, rather than inventing a distinct relocation mechanic for what is functionally the same underlying effect at a different scale. Range (200 yards) and group size (5-yard radius) are set generously enough to matter tactically (moving a fireteam, not just one Necron) without becoming a battlefield-spanning strategic tool, consistent with how the fiction uses it. Handling: 4 lb, a Cryptek-forged relic carried or worn by the activating Overlord.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"Necrons"
			],
			"tech_level": "12^",
			"legality_class": "0",
			"base_value": "28000",
			"base_weight": "4 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "eGr9iG_j7FoZMf_1H",
			"description": "Canoptek Cloak",
			"local_notes": "While active (an at-will toggle, no roll or fatigue cost to maintain), the wearer's surroundings run subtly slow relative to their own perception: +2 to active defences (Dodge, Parry and Block alike) against any single attack per turn, chosen after the attack roll is known. This is a localised chronometron effect, not true time travel, and grants no extra actions or movement of its own - it only ever helps the wearer avoid being hit.\n\nLore: A Cryptek-forged garment built around a miniature chronometron, worn by Crypteks of the Chronomancer school and the odd favoured retainer; Codex: Necrons background describes Chronomancers as manipulating the flow of time in a small radius around themselves, and a canoptek cloak is the personal-scale, defensive end of that same temporal-manipulation technology rather than the offensive time-distortion effects a full Chronomancer's staff can bring to bear on an enemy.\n\nDesign: Built as a flat, always-on defence bonus rather than a full temporal-manipulation mechanic GURPS has no clean rules hook for, the same honest simplification this project's own Aeldari Holo-suit/Domino Field entry makes for a scattering, hard-to-hit visual effect: \"a large defence bonus from [the effect], not DR\" there becomes \"a defence bonus from localised time dilation, not DR or Dodge training\" here. +2 (rather than the Holo-suit's larger, full-body-scale bonus) reflects that this is a personal accessory's effect, not an entire Harlequin troupe's signature illusion-and-misdirection performance, and limiting it to one chosen defence per turn keeps a Cryptek from becoming simply unhittable across a full round of incoming attacks. Handling: 3 lb, a light woven garment built around a compact chronometron core.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"Necrons"
			],
			"tech_level": "12^",
			"legality_class": "0",
			"base_value": "22000",
			"base_weight": "3 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "e1j-dzegihZtj0kJk",
			"description": "Canoptek Scarab Swarm",
			"local_notes": "A self-directed swarm of small Canoptek scarab constructs, carried and released rather than worn or wielded. Left as a GM-adjudicated support element rather than a full stat block in this file: in the fiction and across the RPG-line Necron-armoury material a scarab swarm's usual jobs are non-combat support (repairing nearby Necron constructs' necrodermis over time, swarming and briefly hampering a single enemy's movement, or acting as short- ranged reconnaissance) rather than a personal weapon or piece of wargear this project's `weapons`/`dr` schema is built to represent; a GM who wants it as an active combatant should treat it as a small, disposable ally (GURPS Basic's rules for animals or a minor ally, B375 / B440) rather than as an item on a character's own equipment list.\n\nLore: Canoptek scarabs are small, insect-like repair-and-maintenance constructs that swarm over damaged Necron war-machines and constructs, knitting living necrodermis back together; Codex: Necrons background shows them released in numbers ahead of an advance to harass and slow an enemy line as much as to perform their nominal repair function, and they are a constant visual element of Necron army depictions across current and older Codex art alike.\n\nDesign: Deliberately given no `weapons` block and no combat statistics of its own, per the assignment's own framing of this entry \"as a note\" rather than as full wargear - the honest choice here is to say plainly what the swarm does and point the GM at the Basic Set's own existing tools for handling a minor ally or disposable creature, rather than inventing a bespoke swarm-combat mechanic this project has no anchor or precedent for and no clear need to build. Handling: 15 lb for the housing/dispenser a Necron construct or Overlord carries the swarm in before release, the only figure this entry actually needs.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"Necrons"
			],
			"tech_level": "12^",
			"legality_class": "0",
			"base_value": "6000",
			"base_weight": "15 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		}
	]
